BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7b6342ac57555972@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261008T232032Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261008
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Weiss v. Maine Soapstone Co.
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE            APPELLATE DIVISION \nWORKERS’ COMP
 ENSATION BOARD    Case No. App. Div. 26-0002 \n            Decision No. 26
 -10 \n \n \n \nJEROME WEISS \n(Appellant) \n \nv. \n \nMAINE SOAPSTONE COM
 PANY \, INC. \n(Appellee) \n \nand  \n \nMAINE EMPLOYERS’ MUTUAL INSURAN
 CE COMPANY \n(Insurer) \n  \nDecided: October 8\, 2026 \nConference held: 
 September 2\, 2026 \n \nPANEL MEMBERS: Administrative Law Judges Biddings\
 , Chabot\, and Rooks  \nBy: Administrative Law Judge Biddings \n [¶1]  Je
 rome Weiss appeals from a decision of a Workers’ Compensation \nBoard ad
 ministrative law judge ( Smith\, ALJ) denying his Petition to Reopen for \
 nMistake of Fact or Fraud. Mr. Weiss contends that the ALJ erred in determ
 ining that \nhis petition was untimely under 39-A M.R.S.A. § 321(2)\, and
  in concluding that even \nif the petition had been timely\, Mr. Weiss did
  not establish that Maine Soapstone \nCompany committed fraud. We disagree
  with Mr. Weiss’s contentions and affirm the \ndecision. \n \n \n2 \n \n
 I.  BACKGROUND1 \n [¶2]  Mr. Weiss sustained a work-related shoulder inju
 ry on February 3\, 2009\, \nwhile working for Maine Soapstone  Company. In
 itially\, Maine Soapstone \nvoluntarily paid Mr. Weiss lost time and medic
 al benefits. On September 13\, 2010\, \nMaine Soapstone issued a 21-day le
 tter informing Mr. Weiss and the board of their \nintention to discontinue
  Mr. Weiss’s benefits as of October 5\, 2010. See 39-A \nM.R.S.A. § 205
 (9).  \n[¶3]  In response to the discontinuance of his benefits\, Mr. Wei
 ss filed                  \na Petition for Review \, Request for Provision
 al Order \, and a Petition for Award of \nCompensation regarding the Febru
 ary 3\, 2009\, date of injury. During the litigation\, \nMr. Weiss underwe
 nt an independent medical examination with Dr. Hall. See 39-A \nM.R.S.A. 
 § 312. Dr. Hall opined that the effects of Mr. Weiss’s injury were ongo
 ing \nand that he had retained a partial work capacity. An evidentiary hea
 ring was held on \nMay 16\, 2011\, before a board hearing officer (Pelleti
 er\, HO).2   \n \n  1  Mr. Weiss\nhttps://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/ap
 pellate/2026decisions/26-10_Weiss_v._Maine_Soapstone_Co_10-8-26.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appellate/2026decisions/26-10_We
 iss_v._Maine_Soapstone_Co_10-8-26.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e523ce71e0e485ab@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261008T232015Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261008
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Unemployment Insurance Weekly Claims Report
DESCRIPTION:In the week ending October 3\, the advance figure for seasonal
 ly adjusted initial claims was 197\,000\, a decrease of 2\,000 from the pr
 evious week's revised level. The previous week's level was revised up by 2
 \,000 from 197\,000 to 199\,000. The 4-week moving average was 198\,000\, 
 a decrease of 2\,500 from the previous week's revised average. The previou
 s week's average was revised up by 500 from 200\,000 to 200\,500.\nhttp://
 www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261008
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/eta/eta20261008
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
